BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“The Doctrine” Screening: Gayogo̱hó:nǫˀ Learning Project D
 ecember Film Series
DTSTART:20261206T180000Z
DTEND:20261206T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20261126T185810Z
UID:ce2d83e8ba1ef514094e9e99df17c6ff89f04578@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Film,BIPOC,Community,Connection
DESCRIPTION:On the weekend of December 5 and 6\, the Gayogo̱hó:nǫˀ Lea
 rning Project will present two films\, The Good Mind and The Doctrine\, ea
 ch followed by a panel discussion featuring director Gwendoline Cates\, Ro
 bert Borrero and other Hodinohsó:nih guest speakers. This is a unique opp
 ortunity for audience members to hear firsthand from Native speakers and t
 he experience of making the films.\nThe first film of this weekend program
 \, The Good Mind\, screening one December 5\, is an intimate portrait of t
 he Onondaga Nation\, the Central Fire of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy\, o
 ne of the world’s first and longest-lasting democracies. The film reveal
 s the Nation’s tireless environmental advocacy and their legal battle wi
 th the U.S. over ancestral lands taken by New York State – in violation 
 of a 1794 treaty with George Washington. As an Indigenous sovereign Nation
 \, the Onondaga Nation has never accepted U.S. citizenship\, issues its ow
 n passports\, and maintains a traditional government led by Clanmothers an
 d Chiefs. The film follows Onondaga leaders from the Nation to NYC\, DC an
 d Geneva\, Switzerland\, as they fight for justice.\nThe second film of th
 is program\, The Doctrine\, screening on December 6\, is a feature-length 
 documentary that follows a group of Indigenous youth from Minneapolis who 
 decide to confront officials at the Vatican. The Doctrine of Discovery is 
 the term for decrees issued by 15th-century popes that authorized Christia
 n Europeans’ conquest\, possession\, and exploitation of non-Christian l
 ands\, peoples\, and resources. These edicts codified slavery\, racism\, a
 nd the exploitation of natural resources for profit. They became internati
 onal and US law\, decimating communities of color and driving the current 
 climate crisis. The film also features Indigenous activists in Aotearoa Ne
 w Zealand\, Guatemala\, New Caledonia\, and Puerto Rico (Borikén). At the
  Vatican\, the young people describe from their own experience how the Doc
 trine of Discovery has impacted Indigenous Peoples\, olis by the GLP and o
 ffers an opportunity to engage with Indigenous perspectives and narratives
  across Turtle Island.\nBoth screenings at Cinemapolis in Ithaca\nDec 5th 
 (1pm-3pm): "The Good Mind" Screening: Gayogo̱hó:nǫˀ Learning Project D
 ecember Film Series\nTicket Link\nDec 6th (1pm-4pm): “The Doctrine” Sc
 reening: Gayogo̱hó:nǫˀ Learning Project December Film Series\nTicket L
 ink\n\nSource: FLXcalendar - https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78520
 709/20261206130000
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78520709/20261206130000
END:VEVENT
